## 14. Known Gotchas

**OpenI XML reports sometimes have empty FINDINGS or IMPRESSION fields.** Handle this
in `parse_reports.py` — treat missing fields as empty strings, not errors. Log how many
reports have missing fields before proceeding.

**One report links to multiple images.** Use the first `parentImage` id as the primary
image for the CNN. Optionally log which reports have >2 images (some have frontal + lateral
+ additional views).

**Image IDs in XML must match filenames exactly.** The `parentImage id` attribute is the
filename stem. If a lookup fails, log the missing ID and skip rather than crashing.

**Multi-label loss, not CrossEntropy.** Use `BCEWithLogitsLoss`. Treat `no_finding` as
an independent binary label.

**OpenI is smaller than MIMIC — AUROC targets are lower.** ~4K reports means smaller
train sets and higher variance in metrics. Target macro AUROC ≥ 0.70, not 0.80+.
Report confidence intervals or variance across runs.

**Start with 50–100 samples.** Parse 50–100 XMLs, run the full pipeline, confirm
studies.csv is correct, training loop runs, retrieval returns results, generation
produces valid JSON — then scale to the full dataset.

**Rebuild the FAISS index if the embedding model changes.** The index is model-specific.
Never mix embeddings from different models in the same index.

**gpt-5.4-nano can return markdown fences even when instructed not to.** Always strip before
parsing: `raw.strip().removeprefix("```json").removesuffix("```").strip()`

**Temperature scaling needs its own calibration split.** Do not calibrate on the same
validation set used for model selection. Reserve ~5% of validation for temperature tuning.

**Streamlit reruns the whole script on every interaction.** Wrap the model and FAISS index
in `@st.cache_resource` or the UI will be unusably slow.

**Commit working intermediate states, not just stage completions.** If the XML parser
works but studies.csv isn't finished, commit the parser. Losing working code is painful.

